  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    R = len(grid)\n    C = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find target color\n    target_color = None\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] not in (0, 5, 6):\n                target_color = grid[r][c]\n                break\n        if target_color is not None:\n            break\n            \n    # Find all blocks of target color\n    blocks = []\n    if target_color is not None:\n        for r in range(R):\n            c = 0\n            while c < C:\n                if grid[r][c] == target_color:\n                    c_start = c\n                    while c < C and grid[r][c] == target_color:\n                        c += 1\n                    c_end = c - 1\n                    blocks.append((r, c_start, c_end))\n                else:\n                    c += 1\n                    \n    # Count bullets per column\n    bullets = [0] * C\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] == 6:\n                bullets[c] += 1\n                \n    # Process bullets column by column\n    # Order of columns shouldn't strictly matter for independent columns,\n    # but to be safe, we process columns as they are. \n    # Actually, the bullets all move up simultaneously. If multiple bullets \n    # hit the same block at the exact same time, it might matter. But here \n    # we can process column by column.\n    for c in range(C):\n        for _ in range(bullets[c]):\n            # Find the lowest block intersecting this column\n            hit_block = None\n            max_r = -1\n            for block in blocks:\n                br, c_start, c_end = block\n                if c_start <= c <= c_end:\n                    if br > max_r:\n                        max_r = br\n                        hit_block = block\n            if hit_block is not None:\n                blocks.remove(hit_block)\n                \n    # Build output grid\n    out_grid = [[0] * C for _ in range(R)]\n    for r in range(R):\n        for c in range(C):\n            if grid[r][c] == 5:\n                out_grid[r][c] = 5\n                \n    for br, c_start, c_end in blocks:\n        for c in range(c_start, c_end + 1):\n            out_grid[br][c] = target_color\n            \n    return out_grid",
